the tweets appeared in the name of some  of the most high-profile and powerful  figures in the world  from hundreds of accounts to tens of  millions of followers  and all containing the same message from  the account of former president barack  obama  an apparent offer to double any donation  of a thousand dollars  sent in bitcoin in the words of the  tweet to give back to the community  this was the work of a hack on an  enormous scale  the accounts of celebrities like kim  kardashian were also targeted  an apparent attempt to lure their  followers into nothing more than handing  over money  the corporate accounts of tech giants  like apple were also hit  and even some of the world's richest  figures like the microsoft founder bill  gates  most of the tweets were quickly deleted  as twitter began an investigation  but experts say it's possibly the most  significant hack  that any social media platform has seen  i've never seen anything quite like this  typically  high profile users like this are some of  the most protected accounts on social  media sites  if you can remember a couple years ago  when  a twitter contractor took down donald  trump's account for example that caused  an incredible frenzy now you multiply  that by  i don't know how many accounts were  taken down today but there were  more than 10 that at least we saw and we  tracked some  very high profile people and the fact  that this happened is quite scary i mean  you don't know how this was perpetrated  the targeting of largely democrat  politicians like  presidential candidate joe biden has  raised the specter of the sort of  meddling that plagued  the 2016 u.s election biden's campaign  has asked twitter for answers  the network's founder jack dorsey  tweeted it was a tough day  for us at twitter we all feel terrible  this happened  we're diagnosing and we'll share  everything we can when we have a more  complete understanding  of exactly what happened the company  described the hack as a coordinated  social engineering attack by people who  successfully targeted  some of its employees with access to  systems and tools  they say there could have been other  malicious activity too  it hardly inspired confidence though  that having deleted the fake tweet from  billionaire elon musk  it reappeared not once but twice  twitter's already felt the financial  impact of the crisis in its share price  the hackers though are counting the  profits hundreds of people  did pay up having seen these tweets to  the tune  of more than a hundred thousand dollars  greg milam sky news los  angeles
 

